A||||12||MrPBody||Porting the Duke||||5||
Z||000000||MrPBody||01-12-2001||10:00 PM||||Because I need to replace the head gasket (or maybe the head) on my '85 SE 2.5, I'm considering porting the head and manifold(s).  I have read the Standard Abrasives on-line porting manual, as well as past posts here.  This is new territory to me, so I am looking for informed opinion from someone who has ported a Duke or who understands its head design.  At this stage I am making no other engine modifications, and I will use the car for daily driving.<P>What head/intake/exhaust modifications give best bang for the buck?<P>What parts of the intake passages can I modify with little chance of screwing-up?<P>Does anyone sell valve seat protectors to use while grinding through the valve openings?<P>Does anyone make a higher-performance head compatible with stock manifolds?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||01-13-2001||10:28 AM||||do a search on port and porting there are numerous messages on this. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||01-13-2001||10:38 AM||||Ogre --<P>Most past messages pertain to V6.  By the way, thanks for the Standard Abrasives link.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Smokin87||01-13-2001||02:28 PM||||You can port the heads yourself.  a friend and i are doing it to his car.  if you are using the original head, thats ok, but you'll be left without a car for a while.  if you are buying a new one, then you dont have to worry about being stranded.;<BR>first get you head. then get the head gasket.  place the gasket on the head. with the gasket in the proper place, take a sharpie marker or a scribe tool (use the scribe tool lightly) and draw a line on the inside of the gasket.  this will give you the largest dimentions you can port w/out running into headgasket problems.  after you have scribed the line, use a dremmel tool to port the heads.  but do not go past the line, stay atlest 1/32 or an inch, or 2 mm away from the line.  then when your done, put it all back together and have fun.  but a word to the wise, if you dont feel comfortable doing this yourself, take it to a race shop, at least they will garentee it||||reg||1
Z||000004||Smokin87||01-13-2001||02:30 PM||||also, if you are going to pay someone to do it, take it to a race shop that specializes in GM's like camaros, corvettes, and monte carlos- they are similar||||reg||1
Z||000005||Smokin87||01-13-2001||02:30 PM||||also, if you are going to pay someone to do it, take it to a race shop that specializes in GM's like camaros, corvettes, and monte carlos- they are similar||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||01-13-2001||05:54 PM||||Would the same apply to the exhaust and intake manifolds, laying the gasket on top of it and take out what is blocking the passage?<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||01-13-2001||05:55 PM||||I ported the head on my brothers 87 fiero coupe with the 2.5-liter engine. I used the Standard Abrasives head porting kit to do it. Since then that car has had nothing but fuel problems and it is because I probably did it wrong even though I thought I followed the porting guide to the tee.  <P>The car has less bottom end power than it did before and the top end is only a little better. I don't recommend you do this yourself on an Iron Duke unless you have it professionally done like Smokin87 said. I also don't recommend that you do not port your heads because of the Iron Dukes low 4500 RPM redline. Engines with ported heads usually see horsepower gains in the upper rpm’s where the Iron duke can’t reach. There is allot more to porting heads than just sticking a grinder in there and rounding the sharp edges. It is an art that few people master and easy to screw up. What you are really doing is just cleaning up the flash and smoothing the intake runners, not actually porting the runners, which involves enlarging them. You don’t want to enlarge them.  <P>I probably made the intake bowl area to smooth causing the fuel to drop out of the air charge and puddle in the intake runners because the TBI fuel system is a wet flow system. If you do decide to do this anyway yourself you can use a pair of old valves to protect the seats. If you so much as nick the seat even a little bit you will need a valve job.  Be careful not to make one small scratch in the head gasket surface or you will need to have that resurfaced also. The most power is gained in the bowl area with only a very small amount gained from gasket matching the ports. Take as LITTE out as possible and only smooth out the sharp edges if you do it.  <P>GM performance used to make a high performance cylinder head for this engine but I think you can only use it on the Super Duty engine, which is a heavy duty high performance version of the production engine used in racing cars. <P>If you are using this car for daily driving like you say than don’t do it. You will be sorry you did. <P>You will need a custom prom chip made also. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Smokin87||01-13-2001||09:07 PM||||Yes, the same method would apply for the intake and exhaust manifolds.  but it is true, if the car is used for a daily driver, this process will increce your power, but your gas milege will go down the toilet.  i dont recamend doing this unles the car is strictly a "toy".  and if it a "toy"  then i would say, dont use a Duke.  get a differnt system.  and when it comes time to do the gaskets, get copper gaskets, and nitrous, this will greatly improve power by about 50%.  and the copper gaskets wont blow.<BR>HAVE FUN. and always remember my motto "show those ford boys whos BOSS... GM"||||reg||1
Z||000009||MrPBody||01-13-2001||10:06 PM||||I dig that the Duke is not the best engine to modify, but hey -- it's what I've got.<P>After looking closely at my spare head, I think I won't mess with the combustion chamber much -- maybe smooth a few rough edges, but no major grinding.  Although the head shrouds the intake valve for about 2/3 of its circumferences, I'm guessing that's designed to induce swirling intake flow in the cylinder, and I probably shouldn't disrupt it.<P>The intake valve guide boss occupies a huge chunk of valve throat area.  This seems like an obvious place to grind.  But if it's so obvious, why did GM leave such a lump?  Does it help or hurt?  It's enough to make a guy paranoid about modifying anything.<P>When I grow up and become rich, I'll have lots of spare heads to modify, and a flow bench.  Or maybe I'll just swap for a Quad 4.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||01-13-2001||11:45 PM||||If you've never ported before just do a clean up. This consists of removing lumps and mold flash only. that alone helps things. trying to do to much when you never did one before is a sure way to hose the whole thing.||||reg||1
Z||000011||88formula||01-14-2001||12:49 AM||||That boss supports the alignment of the valves with the seats. If you grind that boss down you will weaken it and end up with excessive valve guide clearance.||||reg||1
Z||000012||MrPBody||01-14-2001||11:20 AM||||<B>Thanks</B> everyone for your advice!<P>I get the message that I should do this with some restraint.<P>I'll let you know how it goes.  If I were a conscientious development engineer -- instead of an engineer wannabe -- I would contact 1FST2M6 for a baseline dyno run, but <I>naaah</I>.<P>Thanks, again.<BR>||||reg||1
